# The three-valued X = Y of intervals with 2-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface",
# "Comparisons"): yes when every member of X equals every member of Y, that is X = Y = [v, v], x_l =
# x_u = y_l = y_u; no when no member of X equals one of Y, the intervals disjoint, x_u < y_l or y_u
# < x_l; both false otherwise and when an operand is empty (the yes of the empty set fails by
# itself: its bounds differ). By the comparisons of two limbs, exact on canonical values, and the
# leading limbs for the test of emptiness. Exact. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_equal_2
input:         xl = xl0 xl1
input:         xu = xu0 xu1
input:         yl = yl0 yl1
input:         yu = yu0 yu1
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      interval yl yu
ensures:       trit yes no eq x y x=xl,xu y=yl,yu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the bounds compared, and a test of emptiness (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical operand

ea  = eq_2_2(xl0, xl1, xu0, xu1)
eb  = eq_2_2(xu0, xu1, yl0, yl1)
ec  = eq_2_2(yl0, yl1, yu0, yu1)
eab = and(ea, eb)
yes = and(eab, ec)
nx  = lt(xl0, const inf)
ny  = lt(yl0, const inf)
ne  = and(nx, ny)
da  = lt_2_2(xu0, xu1, yl0, yl1)
db  = lt_2_2(yu0, yu1, xl0, xl1)
dd  = or(da, db)
no  = and(dd, ne)
